WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.779
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with the president and CEO of Media Matters,

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:06.120
Angelo Carusone.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.599
We're going to talk about, well, many, many
different things.

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:10.800
Angelo, it's great to have you on.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:11.800
I appreciate it.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.800
Speaker 6: Likewise.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:13.800
Thank you.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.680
Speaker 1: So to start with.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.690
There is a feeling that Fox News in particular
and maybe some other right wing media are

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:27.609
starting to set up a little bit of like an
eject button or an escape hatch where Donald

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:29.740
Trump not to be the Republican nominee.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.020
In 2024, Fox News ran like a man on the street
segment where they had a bunch of people saying,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.380
Oh, no, if it was up to me, it would be DeSantis,
who's the nominee.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.880
They're not really against Trump, but it feels
as though they're setting up an escape hatch.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.930
Is that your sense of what's going on?

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.320
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.220
I think that's a really I like the way that
you put it because I a lot of people oftentimes

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.510
put it like they've turned on Trump, right.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.670
Oh, that's that's a little extreme.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.660
But the escape hatch is the right way to think
about it, which is that, you know, one of

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.860
the things that has happened and is that,
you know, in the past, Trump sort of captured

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.159
Fox News, his audience, and was able to really
leapfrog Fox News and speak directly to them.

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:10.560
And then the audience, you know, kind of forced
Fox to align.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.380
That's how he captured I mean, he seized it.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.380
Yeah.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.730
He doesn't have the same tools available to
him.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:18.430
There's opposing candidates like the scientists
that helped create some of that friction.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.290
So what they're basically doing is hedging.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.860
And I will just remind everyone that this
is a very similar posture that Fox News was

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.960
in and poor place in 2015.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.840
You know, in 2013, they had the Fox News primary
where you would have candidates that would

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:33.840
get a ton of attention.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.560
They would be the flavor of the two weeks
and they would shoot to the top.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.250
And there was people criticizing Trump on
air, on network.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:43.930
You know, there was factions within Fox that
had different people that they supported.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.930
And I think that's starting to emerge right
now.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.280
I also think, you know, they're trying to
they're hedging because it's not clear that

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.900
Trump is going to be able to replicate what
they did in 20 in 2016, and they kind of know

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:55.900
it.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.039
So escape hatch is exactly the right way to.

00:01:57.039 --> 00:01:59.570
Speaker 1: Put it to go back to 2015, 2016.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.980
There was that early period where Fox was
very much not on the Trump train.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.430
And as you say, ultimately they did acquiesce.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:11.310
Is there anything about the back story of
the relationship between Trump and the Murdochs

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.060
that is relevant to how that went?

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.400
Or was that not a factor in your mind?

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.160
Speaker 6: It was not so much of a factor.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.280
It was really about Roger Ailes.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.599
The only thing that was relevant at the time
about the Murdochs is that Rupert Murdoch

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:29.739
has always wanted Fox to really have a president,
like not to just have an alliance with the

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:30.739
president.

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:34.500
You know, they were obviously aligned with
the Bush administration, but there was a they

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.420
were two different entities that sort of had
some synergy.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.140
I mean, what Murdoch has always fantasized
about has been to really create a president

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.360
or, you know, a world leader, but really a
president.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:52.690
And so what when Ailes started to put more
of the Fox News juice behind Trump, as things

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.990
started to seem to break in his favor, Murdoch
really saw that as an opportunity to create

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:58.990
something.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.940
And that's where he sort of said, okay, I'm
all in now.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.459
And then he cashed in on those favors as soon
as Trump got into office, right?

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:08.750
By doing some pretty outrageous things, trying
to get him to slow down the deal so that he

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.310
could come up with and maybe even buy CNN.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.840
And, you know, it really that was the determining
factor.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.670
But it wasn't until after it was pretty clear
that Trump could capture the nomination that

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.400
Murdoch said, okay, we can go all in now.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.769
Speaker 1: Before the FBI search at Mar a
Lago, there was this stretch of time where

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:29.460
Trump was just gone from Fox News.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.430
He was doing and still is doing interviews
where oftentimes he's just literally phoning

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.390
it in channels that nobody's heard of.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.670
And, you know, it seemed as though maybe this
was a sign that his star is fading after the

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:41.670
search.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.849
That was the entire the Sean HANNITY interview,
which, by the way, was a total, total disaster.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:46.791
It didn't really do anything good for him.

00:03:46.791 --> 00:03:48.590
But put that aside for a second.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.090
Was there something to that absence from Fox
News in your mind?

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.650
Speaker 6: There was.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:58.470
Now, they still talked about him 9 to 10,
15 times more than anybody else.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.110
Yeah, but there was something to it.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:01.110
Two things, actually.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.950
One was that was the beginning of the January
six hearings.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:10.110
And there was all of this information coming
out, testimony where, you know, there was

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.800
actually, you know, Fox News hosts were part
of it where they were actually texting and

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:14.800
talking.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:15.939
So it complicated things a little bit.

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:20.419
And then the second thing and this is really
important is that the defamation lawsuit that

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:24.970
from Dominion, which is that voting machine
company that's suing Fox News for $1,000,000,000,

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:30.410
they had been arguing for almost seven, eight
months that the Murdochs would not be part

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.750
of it.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:37.290
And just in the beginning of the summer, it
was that the courts ruled that actually the

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.669
Murdochs will be a part of this litigation.

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:44.419
And what that meant is that Fox executives
in the Murdochs were trying to demonstrate

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:48.410
that, oh, there's some distance between us
and Trump.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:52.210
And there was a little bit of back and forth
over that summer as they started to calibrate

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.310
their legal strategy.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.570
So I think it was really those two things
coming together, which is let's keep them

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.521
at arm's length because every time he appears
on our network, he's either it's either going

00:05:00.521 --> 00:05:05.389
to create a new round of stories about our
hosts from January six, or it's going to complicate

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:08.889
the back end stuff that we're dealing with
with this massive billion dollar lawsuit.

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:13.389
Speaker 1: So I want to get off FOX after
one more question and get on to some other

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:14.480
right wing media outlets.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.350
But at this point, do you have a sense of
who's more powerful within the Republican

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:23.819
base in terms of Trump or Fox News, in the
sense of if Trump says, I'm running and Fox

00:05:23.819 --> 00:05:29.140
did decide we actually want to prevent Trump
from being the nominee, who is the power right

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.140
now?

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.140
Speaker 6: Tucker.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.140
Speaker 1: Tucker.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.140
Okay.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.170
Speaker 6: Specifically, Tucker is just Tucker.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:39.750
And so the rest of Fox would have to would
break for Trump at this point.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:44.000
Fox would be able to capture Trump would be
able to hijack the Fox audience.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.570
And we're seeing that play out a little bit
right now with the attacks on Pelosi, because

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:52.110
it has been, you know, Fox initially treated
like a traditional crime story and then after

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:56.889
sort of the Trump part of the ecosystem was
weaving conspiracies, all of a sudden Fox

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.569
News sort of broke and went with the conspiracy
direction.

00:05:59.569 --> 00:06:01.949
So there's lots of indicators like that.

00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:05.260
So if Fox was against Trump, Trump would win
that fight.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:11.009
But if Tucker is, then Trump is in trouble
because Tucker really has replaced Limbaugh

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:15.430
as sort of like the what he used to describe
as the titular head of the Republican Party.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.090
Tucker has really filled that space.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.220
So Trump v fox.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.220
Trump Trump v Tucker.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:26.210
Speaker 1: Tucker what do you make of recently
even Newsmax?

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:32.130
So the way I put some of these on a spectrum
is you've got Fox, which is on the right,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:36.330
and then you've got Newsmax, which is crazier
than Fox, and then you've got Oann, which

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.060
is crazier than Newsmax that I sort of have
them like that.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.240
And you can tell me whether you agree or disagree.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.120
Newsmax recently is not even taking all of
Donald Trump's rallies.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.860
They still take most of them, but not all
of them.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:53.349
Does that mean that even within the even crazier
wing, maybe Trump's star is fading?

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:55.310
Speaker 6: A little bit.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:07:00.500
And it's also you know, it's a little complicated,
too, because they are some other entities

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.460
went out there and really cornered that market
of the rallies live.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:08.250
So, you know, Trump's own lawyer hosts the
right side broadcasting.

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.250
Right.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:12.250
I mean, when they're so yeah, I mean, they
have an inside track.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:16.849
Fox News actually has an entire channel on
their Fox Nation app that they will give away

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:17.889
free trials for.

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:21.199
So they use the Trump rallies to get more
people to sign up.

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:27.110
The entire section is devoted to just Trump's
rallies, so they kind of others got into the

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:28.630
space to try to commercialize it.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:33.350
So I think there's a financial like cornering
the market factor.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:37.220
But I look at the metrics, you know, they
see that, you know, the rallies don't have

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.870
the same juice and star power that they did
in the past.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:44.349
And for Newsmax, which has slightly thinner
margins, it doesn't get them the same bang

00:07:44.349 --> 00:07:45.349
for the buck.

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:47.780
And also every time he says something about
the election, they have to worry about their

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.129
channels and getting zapped.

00:07:49.129 --> 00:07:51.780
And so for them, it's just it's just not worth
it.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:59.280
Speaker 1: So you talked a little bit about
the Trump versus someone else primary in terms

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.420
of Tucker and the power dynamics there.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:06.919
But if we zoom out a little and even like
separate it maybe from from from Fox News,

00:08:06.919 --> 00:08:12.319
how do you believe right wing media more generally
will handle if it's Trump?

00:08:12.319 --> 00:08:17.849
DeSantis Because I don't think DeSantis can
be written off as a RINO in the same way that

00:08:17.849 --> 00:08:20.220
they do with like Mitt Romney, for example.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:23.449
So what do you think they're going to come
up with for covering such a primary?

00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:25.300
Speaker 6: It's going to be really hard.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:26.419
There's two complicating factor.

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:28.069
There's two big considerations.

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:34.360
One is that DeSantis has a pretty potent piece
of like kryptonite that he started to deploy,

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:35.539
which is vaccines.

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:37.469
And it started over the summer.

00:08:37.469 --> 00:08:42.539
And he started to basically, you know, he's
been apologizing for saying that he he was

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:43.539
misled.

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:47.980
He should have never promoted the vaccines
is probably the only wedge where he can really

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:54.040
successfully outflank Trump to the right and
have credibility with the right wing media.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:55.040
And that's what it's about.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.730
It's about sort of hijacking the right wing,
outflanking Trump when it comes to some of

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.290
these right wing personalities to say, look
at the Santos, you know, he's willing to accept

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:05.420
the fact that he should have and he's been
anti-vax and he's anti state control and we're

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:11.000
free, you know, liberty and you know, he did
it a little bit over the summer and it worked.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.570
It changed the way right wing media talked
about them.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:17.230
That's when Alex Jones started to really hit
Donald Trump was on the vaccine stuff.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.790
And then you could look at a bunch of these
right wing media hosts that were saying, Oh,

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.320
Trump's going to have to grapple with this
at some point.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.910
I mean, why won't he just say he was lied
to?

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.760
Why won't he apologize about this?

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:26.760
He's so stubborn.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:30.779
And dissent has started to, you know, basically
deploy it as a trial balloon, did it for about

00:09:30.779 --> 00:09:32.050
six weeks and he walked it back.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:36.870
So I think one is how heavily he leans into
that and how Trump navigates that is a weakness

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:41.010
because it's Trump's base is not with Trump
on this, and that is this weird tension where

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.320
he's out of step with them.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:44.420
And so that's one.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:48.640
And I think that will affect the way the media
lines up, because it will give them cover.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.250
To then criticize Trump in a way that doesn't
get them blowback from their own audiences

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.430
is to get him on that terrain.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.130
So that's that's one part.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:10:01.420
And then the other part is, is actually a
knock against a is that dissent is is no fun.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:04.459
So the truth is, nobody really likes him,
right?

00:10:04.459 --> 00:10:05.470
That's what everyone's hedging on.

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:07.000
And I'm not trying to be mean or glib about.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.000
It's the truth.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.160
He really is no fun.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.720
Even his closest allies acknowledge that he's
genuinely no fun.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.570
He's aloof, he's not charismatic.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:15.570
He's.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:20.649
And if you're in the media, you need to think
about the product and what's the sustaining

00:10:20.649 --> 00:10:21.920
and staying power.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.650
And that's the challenge with DeSantis, is
that I think from the media perspective, they're

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:32.110
all aware of the fact that DeSantis doesn't
have the same sort of regenerative power in

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.279
terms of media consumption in the way that
that Trump does.

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:40.180
So it's this weird thing, but I do think the
vaccine piece is going to really be challenging

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:43.860
and Trump is going to take a lot of flak for
that from the right wing media.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.370
And if they want to jump, that's going to
be their launchpad.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.920
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask you about.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:53.870
What is the timing of the 2024 primary getting
going?

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:57.650
Because some part of me thinks it gets going
on November 9th, quite frankly.

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.079
Speaker 6: Yeah, I think you're right.

00:11:00.079 --> 00:11:04.240
I have always been pointing to the fact that
there's no way that Trump would announce a

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.829
candidacy until his airplane was ready.

00:11:06.829 --> 00:11:10.149
And it was supposed to be ready back in July.

00:11:10.149 --> 00:11:13.810
But of course, because of all the supply chain
issues, pieces of it weren't complete and

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.230
the timeline got radically disrupted.

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:17.279
It is now officially finished.

00:11:17.279 --> 00:11:19.530
There's no way that he would run for president
without his plane.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:22.579
It's too and it's too much of an important
part of the staging.

00:11:22.579 --> 00:11:23.579
Right.

00:11:23.579 --> 00:11:25.600
And so it's just it was an obvious to me.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.980
It just felt very obvious that he couldn't
be a candidate without his plane.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:30.820
It doesn't work.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:36.020
And and so that's to me, if this plane wasn't
ready, I would say we have to wait.

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:38.451
But because his plane is now ready, I agree
with you.

00:11:38.451 --> 00:11:41.649
I think it's going to start almost immediately
after November 9th.

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:45.721
And that ties into what's happening now, because
a lot of what's happening on the ground, the

00:11:45.721 --> 00:11:50.160
infrastructure at the state level, a lot of
the organizations, the the cluster of groups

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.310
that are going to get power or potentially
get power if the election goes in their favor.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:58.540
This is all part of the Trump ecosystem that
has been building this groundwork.

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:03.730
So it's going to almost be at the apex and
a more and a lot of momentum for him to make

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:05.589
a quick jump.

00:12:05.589 --> 00:12:09.740
And he's also seen to the extent that they're
going to challenge any elections, he is really

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:12.100
the face of that strategy and approach.

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:15.699
So I think you're right, it's going to start
very, very, very early.

00:12:15.699 --> 00:12:18.910
Speaker 1: And it sounds like from the language
you're using, you have no doubt whatsoever

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:20.010
that Trump is running.

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:21.590
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.390
I think he's going to run for sure.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.029
I do.

00:12:25.029 --> 00:12:27.880
I don't think there's any reason to stop him
from running.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:31.620
And he is fueled in large part by spite and
revenge.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:36.399
And every measurable indicator still shows
that he could succeed in the primary.

00:12:36.399 --> 00:12:41.269
